{"module":"media","voice":"female","duration":159.0,"segments":[{"el":"title","text":"Media and image-making","start":0.0,"end":1.99,"words":[[0.06,0.61],[0.61,0.89],[0.89,1.91]]},{"el":"intro","text":"How people are shown shapes how they're treated, including who a caller sees as “suspicious.”","start":2.79,"end":7.94,"words":[[2.83,3.04],[3.04,3.39],[3.39,3.46],[3.46,3.74],[3.74,4.09],[4.09,4.3],[4.3,4.58],[4.58,5.08],[5.32,5.88],[5.88,6.03],[6.03,6.17],[6.17,6.45],[6.45,6.66],[6.66,6.8],[6.8,7.43]]},{"el":"h0","text":"Minstrelsy and “Jim Crow”","start":8.29,"end":10.17,"words":[[8.34,9.17],[9.17,9.42],[9.42,9.75],[9.75,10.09]]},{"el":"s0","text":"In the 1830s white performer Thomas D.","start":10.87,"end":14.2,"words":[[10.93,11.1],[11.1,11.28],[11.28,12.15],[12.15,12.49],[12.49,13.01],[13.01,13.45],[13.45,13.71]]},{"el":"s0","text":"Rice blacked up to play a character he called “Jim Crow,” and minstrel shows became America's most popular entertainment for decades.","start":14.55,"end":22.26,"words":[[14.6,14.88],[14.88,15.22],[15.22,15.36],[15.36,15.49],[15.49,15.77],[15.77,15.9],[15.9,16.38],[16.38,16.52],[16.52,16.79],[16.79,17.07],[17.07,17.41],[17.41,17.62],[17.62,18.16],[18.16,18.44],[18.44,18.85],[18.85,19.4],[19.4,19.74],[19.74,20.22],[20.22,21.04],[21.04,21.18],[21.18,21.72]]},{"el":"s0","text":"The character's name later became the name for the whole system of segregation laws.","start":22.61,"end":27.67,"words":[[22.65,22.8],[22.8,23.38],[23.38,23.67],[23.67,24.04],[24.04,24.47],[24.47,24.62],[24.62,24.91],[24.91,25.05],[25.05,25.2],[25.2,25.49],[25.49,25.93],[25.93,26.07],[26.07,26.88],[26.88,27.17]]},{"el":"s0","text":"Caricatures sold products for more than a century; the Aunt Jemima brand was only retired in 2020–2021.","start":28.02,"end":35.5,"words":[[28.02,28.73],[28.73,29.05],[29.05,29.57],[29.57,29.7],[29.7,29.82],[29.82,30.02],[30.02,30.15],[30.15,30.72],[30.95,31.08],[31.08,31.27],[31.27,31.72],[31.72,32.04],[32.04,32.24],[32.24,32.56],[32.56,33.01],[33.01,33.14],[33.14,35.07]]},{"el":"h1","text":"The Birth of a Nation","start":36.4,"end":37.75,"words":[[36.44,36.6],[36.6,36.84],[36.84,37.0],[37.0,37.16],[37.16,37.64]]},{"el":"s1","text":"D.W.","start":38.45,"end":39.39,"words":[[38.5,39.01]]},{"el":"s1","text":"Griffith's 1915 film portrayed Reconstruction's Black officeholders as menacing and the Ku Klux Klan as heroes.","start":39.74,"end":47.45,"words":[[39.79,40.31],[40.31,41.28],[41.28,41.58],[41.58,42.11],[42.11,43.16],[43.16,43.45],[43.45,44.28],[44.28,44.43],[44.43,44.95],[44.95,45.18],[45.18,45.33],[45.33,45.47],[45.47,45.85],[45.85,46.15],[46.15,46.3],[46.3,46.9]]},{"el":"s1","text":"It was screened at the White House.","start":47.8,"end":49.74,"words":[[47.85,47.96],[47.96,48.14],[48.14,48.49],[48.49,48.6],[48.6,48.72],[48.72,48.95],[48.95,49.24]]},{"el":"s1","text":"The NAACP protested it nationwide.","start":50.09,"end":53.16,"words":[[50.14,50.27],[50.27,51.18],[51.18,51.81],[51.81,51.95],[51.95,52.71]]},{"el":"s1","text":"That November, a new Ku Klux Klan was founded at Stone Mountain, Georgia, and grew to millions of members in the 1920s.","start":53.51,"end":62.53,"words":[[53.55,53.78],[53.78,54.45],[54.71,54.86],[54.86,55.09],[55.09,55.24],[55.24,55.61],[55.61,55.91],[55.91,56.13],[56.13,56.66],[56.66,56.81],[56.81,57.18],[57.18,57.78],[58.04,58.56],[58.83,59.05],[59.05,59.28],[59.28,59.42],[59.42,59.95],[59.95,60.1],[60.1,60.55],[60.55,60.7],[60.7,60.85],[60.85,61.97]]},{"el":"h2","text":"Crime news","start":63.43,"end":64.5,"words":[[63.48,63.97],[63.97,64.35]]},{"el":"s2","text":"The 1968 Kerner Commission, appointed after urban uprisings, criticized news organizations for reporting “from the standpoint of a white man's world.”","start":65.2,"end":75.64,"words":[[65.25,65.4],[65.4,66.67],[66.67,66.96],[66.96,67.56],[67.82,68.42],[68.42,68.71],[68.71,69.01],[69.01,69.76],[70.02,70.76],[70.76,71.06],[71.06,72.03],[72.03,72.18],[72.18,72.7],[72.7,73.0],[73.0,73.15],[73.15,73.89],[73.89,74.04],[74.04,74.19],[74.19,74.49],[74.49,74.78],[74.78,75.16]]},{"el":"s2","text":"Studies of local TV news have found Black people shown as criminal suspects more often than arrest data would predict.","start":75.99,"end":83.23,"words":[[76.01,76.45],[76.45,76.6],[76.6,77.04],[77.04,77.34],[77.34,77.63],[77.63,77.85],[77.85,78.22],[78.22,78.52],[78.52,78.89],[78.89,79.18],[79.18,79.33],[79.33,79.92],[79.92,80.51],[80.51,80.66],[80.66,80.95],[80.95,81.17],[81.17,81.54],[81.54,81.91],[81.91,82.13],[82.13,82.72]]},{"el":"s2","text":"In 1989 five Black and Latino teenagers were convicted in the Central Park jogger case after intense coverage and a full-page newspaper ad calling for the death penalty; they were exonerated in 2002.","start":83.58,"end":98.24,"words":[[83.63,83.79],[83.79,85.0],[85.0,85.33],[85.33,85.65],[85.65,85.89],[85.89,86.46],[86.46,87.18],[87.18,87.34],[87.34,88.07],[88.07,88.23],[88.23,88.39],[88.39,88.96],[88.96,89.2],[89.2,89.6],[89.6,89.92],[89.92,90.25],[90.25,90.73],[90.73,91.38],[91.38,91.62],[91.62,91.78],[91.78,92.42],[92.42,93.15],[93.15,93.31],[93.31,93.72],[93.72,93.88],[93.88,94.04],[94.04,94.28],[94.28,94.92],[95.21,95.45],[95.45,95.61],[95.61,96.58],[96.58,96.74],[96.74,97.71]]},{"el":"s2","text":"In 1995 political scientist John DiIulio warned of coming juvenile “superpredators”; the wave never came, and he later expressed regret.","start":98.59,"end":108.81,"words":[[98.64,98.78],[98.78,100.03],[100.03,100.69],[100.69,101.35],[101.35,101.64],[101.64,102.44],[102.44,102.74],[102.74,102.88],[102.88,103.25],[103.25,103.91],[103.91,104.86],[105.12,105.27],[105.27,105.56],[105.56,105.85],[105.85,106.22],[106.47,106.69],[106.69,106.84],[106.84,107.21],[107.21,107.79],[107.79,108.31]]},{"el":"h3","text":"How much does coverage change minds?","start":109.71,"end":112.01,"words":[[109.73,109.9],[109.9,110.13],[110.13,110.3],[110.3,110.75],[110.75,111.15],[111.15,111.55]]},{"el":"s3","text":"Experiments have found that viewers shown a Black suspect in a news story become more supportive of punitive policies than viewers shown a white suspect or none.","start":112.71,"end":122.98,"words":[[112.77,113.75],[113.75,114.0],[114.0,114.4],[114.4,114.65],[114.65,115.05],[115.05,115.38],[115.38,115.54],[115.54,115.87],[115.87,116.44],[116.44,116.6],[116.6,116.77],[116.77,117.09],[117.09,117.5],[117.5,117.91],[117.91,118.07],[118.07,118.64],[118.64,118.8],[118.8,119.46],[119.46,120.03],[120.03,120.27],[120.27,120.68],[120.68,121.0],[121.0,121.17],[121.17,121.49],[121.49,122.06],[122.06,122.15],[122.15,122.47]]},{"el":"s3","text":"Researchers debate how large and lasting these effects are outside the lab.","start":123.33,"end":127.55,"words":[[123.37,123.92],[123.92,124.33],[124.33,124.54],[124.54,124.81],[124.81,125.02],[125.02,125.44],[125.44,125.64],[125.64,126.06],[126.06,126.12],[126.12,126.61],[126.61,126.75],[126.75,127.02]]},{"el":"h4","text":"Cameras that turned the story","start":128.45,"end":130.17,"words":[[128.47,128.95],[128.95,129.19],[129.19,129.52],[129.52,129.68],[129.68,130.08]]},{"el":"s4","text":"Ida B. Wells used reporting to expose lynching.","start":130.87,"end":134.5,"words":[[130.92,131.2],[131.2,131.41],[131.76,132.04],[132.04,132.32],[132.32,132.82],[132.82,132.96],[132.96,133.45],[133.45,134.01]]},{"el":"s4","text":"In 1955 Mamie Till-Mobley insisted on an open casket for her son Emmett, and Jet published the photographs.","start":134.85,"end":142.86,"words":[[134.9,135.06],[135.06,136.28],[136.28,136.67],[136.67,137.28],[137.28,137.89],[137.89,138.04],[138.04,138.2],[138.2,138.58],[138.58,139.04],[139.04,139.19],[139.19,139.35],[139.35,139.57],[139.57,139.96],[140.23,140.46],[140.46,140.76],[140.76,141.3],[141.3,141.45],[141.45,142.29]]},{"el":"s4","text":"In 2020, 17-year-old Darnella Frazier recorded George Floyd's murder; she received a special Pulitzer citation in 2021.","start":143.21,"end":152.08,"words":[[143.26,143.4],[143.4,144.26],[144.49,145.56],[145.56,145.95],[145.